\image(figures/xgb_iris_data.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The data, species summaries and reproducible stratified partition sizes. The full-data scatter is used only to describe the measurements.)
\alttext(A petal-length versus petal-width scatter coloured by species. Setosa is separate, while versicolor and virginica partly overlap.)

Each class contributes 30 training, 10 validation and 10 final test flowers.
The outer split reserves 20% with seed 42; the inner split reserves 25%
of the remaining development data with seed 43. Both splits preserve
original row order after selection. We predeclare depth two, learning rate
0.1, full row/column sampling, at most 100 rounds and the lambda grid
\(\{0.01,0.1,1,10,100\}\). Validation chooses among these settings;
the final test data do not participate in that choice. No claim about
relative performance against classifiers fitted with different defaults
is made here.

> Deriving a regularised boosting round

For class scores \(F_{ik}\), probabilities and the mean negative log
likelihood are

$$
p_{ik}=\frac{e^{F_{ik}}}{\sum_j e^{F_{ij}}},\qquad
\mathcal L=-\frac1n\sum_i\log p_{i,y_i}.
$$
\alttext(Softmax converts class scores into probabilities. Mean log loss is the average negative logarithm of the probability assigned to the observed class.)

The initial scores are log training-class proportions. Subtracting each
row's largest score before exponentiating leaves probabilities unchanged
and stabilises the computation. Differentiating the per-row loss gives

$$
g_{ik}=p_{ik}-\mathbf1\{y_i=k\},\qquad
\frac{\partial^2\ell_i}{\partial F_{ik}\partial F_{ij}}
=p_{ik}(\mathbf1\{k=j\}-p_{ij}).
$$
\alttext(The gradient is predicted probability minus the class indicator. The full softmax Hessian has diagonal p times one minus p and nonzero off-diagonal entries minus the product of class probabilities.)

The local implementation retains only the diagonal
\(h_{ik}=\max(p_{ik}(1-p_{ik}),10^{-6})\). It computes all class
gradients from the scores at the start of a round, fits one tree per class,
then adds the learning-rate-scaled contributions. Omitting off-diagonal
terms makes this a diagonal Newton heuristic; it is not an exact joint
Newton step. XGBoost 3.1.0's upstream multiclass objective instead uses the
bound \(2p(1-p)\) with its own floor. Identical option names therefore
do not establish numerical equivalence with that package.

>> Leaf weights and split gains

For a leaf, sum gradients and Hessians over its assigned training rows:
\(G=\sum_i g_i\), \(H=\sum_i h_i\). With L2 penalty
\(\lambda\) and L1 penalty \(\alpha\), its quadratic surrogate is

$$
Q(w)=Gw+\frac12(H+\lambda)w^2+\alpha|w|,\qquad
w^*=-\frac{\operatorname{sign}(G)\max(|G|-\alpha,0)}{H+\lambda}.
$$
\alttext(The leaf objective combines the linear gradient term, curvature plus L2 penalty, and an L1 absolute-value penalty. Its minimum is the negative soft-thresholded gradient divided by Hessian plus lambda.)

For positive or negative \(w\), set the corresponding derivative to
zero. At \(w=0\), the subgradient condition is \(|G|\le\alpha\),
which yields exactly zero. This derives the soft-threshold rule. Increasing
lambda shrinks a leaf update for fixed \(G,H\), but later gradients and
chosen splits also change, so an entire fitted model need not vary
monotonically with lambda.

Put \(S(G,H)=\operatorname{softthresh}(G,\alpha)^2/(H+\lambda)\).
The best surrogate contribution is \(-S/2\). Subtracting the children's
best objective from the parent's and charging a split penalty gives

$$
\operatorname{Gain}=\frac12\{S(G_L,H_L)+S(G_R,H_R)-S(G,H)\}-\gamma.
$$
\alttext(The split gain is half the two child scores minus the parent score, then minus gamma. Positive gain represents improvement in the regularised quadratic surrogate.)

The local tree enumerates exact midpoint thresholds and requires child
row-count and summed-Hessian limits. A gain must improve the current best
by more than \(10^{-12}\). Gain importance sums accepted regularised split
gains, then normalises across features; it is training-derived attribution,
not a causal effect or a probability that a predictor matters. A smaller
learning rate and more rounds can compensate for shrinkage, so a fixed
round budget is part of any regularisation comparison.

The source's `training_deviance()` is mean negative log likelihood, not
twice that quantity. Evaluation uses the same definition and a probability
floor of \(10^{-15}\) for the logarithm. Brier score is
\(n^{-1}\sum_i\sum_k(p_{ik}-\mathbf1\{y_i=k\})^2\), without dividing
by the number of classes. Balanced three-class priors consequently give
mean log loss \(\log3\), Brier score \(2/3\), and tie-broken accuracy
\(1/3\). Probabilities are fitted softmax outputs, not verified calibrated
posteriors.

> Training and validation curves from the same fitted trees

For illustration, first fix lambda at one and fit the entire 100-round
budget. `staged_evaluation()` traverses that model's fitted trees once in
round order; it never trains on the evaluation frame or refits a separate
model at every stage. Round zero evaluates the original training priors.
Counts explicitly record rows omitted for null predictors or response.
The iris data have no omitted rows.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/xgb_iris_paths.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  const auto [development,test] = dm::stats::train_test_split(dm::datasets::iris(),0.2,42,"Species");
  const auto [train,validation] = dm::stats::train_test_split(development,0.25,43,"Species");
  dm::stats::XGBoostClassifierOptions options; options.n_trees=100; options.max_depth=2; options.learning_rate=0.1;
  const dm::stats::XGBoostClassifier model(train,"Species ~ .",options);
  const auto training = model.staged_evaluation(train), valid = model.staged_evaluation(validation);
  std::print("Validation stages:\n\n{}\n",valid.take_rows({0,10,20,50,100}).to_md(6));
  std::print("\nTraining final stage:\n\n{}\n",training.tail(1).to_md(6));
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  dm::graphics::RPlot::create().line(training.get_column("round").values(),training.get_column("mean_log_loss").values(),
      "training",dm::RGB{37,99,235},2)
      .line(valid.get_column("round").values(),valid.get_column("mean_log_loss").values(),"validation",dm::RGB{220,38,38},2)
      .title("Fixed lambda = 1: training loss and validation loss diverge")
      .x_label("completed boosting rounds").y_label("mean log loss")
      .size(1000,480).save_svg("build/xgb_iris_paths.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Validation stages:

| round | input_rows | evaluated_rows | skipped_rows | accuracy | mean_log_loss | brier |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 0 | 30 | 30 | 0 | 0.333333 | 1.098612 | 0.666667 |
| 10 | 30 | 30 | 0 | 0.933333 | 0.285492 | 0.119005 |
| 20 | 30 | 30 | 0 | 0.933333 | 0.234883 | 0.107851 |
| 50 | 30 | 30 | 0 | 0.933333 | 0.259089 | 0.115874 |
| 100 | 30 | 30 | 0 | 0.933333 | 0.255237 | 0.113452 |


Training final stage:

| round | input_rows | evaluated_rows | skipped_rows | accuracy | mean_log_loss | brier |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 100 | 90 | 90 | 0 | 1 | 0.038867 | 0.006592 |

\image(figures/xgb_iris_paths.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Staged losses for a model fitted to 90 training flowers. The validation labels only score fixed trees. These curves do not use the reserved final test partition.)
\alttext(Two log-loss curves falling from the three-class prior baseline. Training loss keeps declining while validation loss levels off and rises modestly.)

>> Selecting lambda and the stopping round on validation only

For each predeclared lambda, the library picks the lowest validation mean
log loss among stages 1 through 100. It then compares those five candidates.
Exact ties retain the first supplied lambda and the earliest stage. The
round-zero prior is a useful reference but excluded from this search.
Validation accuracy can be identical while probability-sensitive log loss
differs; accuracy alone would conceal that distinction.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/xgb_iris_selection.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  const auto [development,test] = dm::stats::train_test_split(dm::datasets::iris(),0.2,42,"Species");
  const auto [train,validation] = dm::stats::train_test_split(development,0.25,43,"Species");
  dm::stats::XGBoostClassifierOptions options; options.n_trees=100; options.max_depth=2; options.learning_rate=0.1;
  const auto search = dm::stats::xgboost_validation_search(train,validation,"Species ~ .",options);
  std::print("{}\n",search.candidates.to_md(6));
  std::print("\nSelected lambda: {}; selected rounds: {}.\n",search.selected_options.lambda,search.selected_options.n_trees);
  auto log_lambda = search.candidates.get_column("lambda").values();
  for (auto& value : log_lambda) value = std::log10(value);
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  dm::graphics::RPlot::create().line(log_lambda,search.candidates.get_column("mean_log_loss").values(),
      "validation loss",dm::RGB{37,99,235},2)
      .points(log_lambda,search.candidates.get_column("mean_log_loss").values(),"",dm::RGB{37,99,235},5)
      .title("Best validation loss per lambda within 100 rounds")
      .x_label("log10(lambda)").y_label("best validation mean log loss")
      .size(1000,480).save_svg("build/xgb_iris_selection.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| lambda | round | evaluated_rows | accuracy | mean_log_loss | brier |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 0.01 | 13 | 30 | 0.933333 | 0.241081 | 0.104946 |
| 0.10 | 13 | 30 | 0.933333 | 0.235788 | 0.103294 |
| 1.00 | 17 | 30 | 0.933333 | 0.231535 | 0.104302 |
| 10.00 | 57 | 30 | 0.933333 | 0.221657 | 0.101410 |
| 100.00 | 100 | 30 | 0.933333 | 0.350397 | 0.147607 |


Selected lambda: 10; selected rounds: 57.
\image(figures/xgb_iris_selection.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Each lambda's best stage and validation scores. The stopping round varies across candidates; this is a joint selection over lambda and stage, not a fixed-stage lambda curve.)
\alttext(A validation-loss curve over five logarithmically spaced penalties, with its lowest point at lambda ten and a higher loss at lambda one hundred.)

The selected configuration is lambda 10 and 57 rounds. The lambda-100
candidate reaches its best reported value at the budget boundary, so this
experiment does not establish its best possible performance with more
rounds. The selected validation loss is optimistically biased by the
search itself. It is not substituted for the final test score.

> Refit on development data, then evaluate the final test partition

The selected settings are refitted from scratch on all 120 development
flowers, combining the training and validation partitions. This can change
every fitted tree; the selected validation loss does not describe the
refitted model directly. The 30 reserved flowers are evaluated after that
refit. The feature-importance plot uses only the development-fitted trees.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/xgb_iris_test.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  const auto [development,test] = dm::stats::train_test_split(dm::datasets::iris(),0.2,42,"Species");
  const auto [train,validation] = dm::stats::train_test_split(development,0.25,43,"Species");
  dm::stats::XGBoostClassifierOptions options; options.n_trees=100; options.max_depth=2; options.learning_rate=0.1;
  const auto search = dm::stats::xgboost_validation_search(train,validation,"Species ~ .",options);
  const dm::stats::XGBoostClassifier final_model(development,"Species ~ .",search.selected_options);
  std::print("Final test:\n\n{}\n\n{}\n",final_model.evaluation(test).to_md(6),final_model.confusion(test).to_md());
  std::print("\n| training flowers | training accuracy | training mean log loss |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: |\n"
      "| {} | {:.6f} | {:.6f} |\n",final_model.observations(),final_model.training_accuracy(),final_model.training_deviance().back());
  auto layout = dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1,2);
  layout.add(final_model.plot_classification(test,"Petal.Length","Petal.Width")
      .title("Final test: four-feature predictions").size(750,500));
  layout.add(dm::graphics::RPlot::barplot(final_model.feature_importance(),final_model.predictor_names(),"gain share")
      .title("Development-fit split gains").y_label("normalised gain").size(750,500));
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");layout.size(1500,500).save_svg("build/xgb_iris_test.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Final test:

| round | input_rows | evaluated_rows | skipped_rows | accuracy | mean_log_loss | brier |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 57 | 30 | 30 | 0 | 0.933333 | 0.223922 | 0.126820 |


| actual | pred_setosa | pred_versicolor | pred_virginica |
| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| setosa | 10 | 0 | 0 |
| versicolor | 0 | 9 | 1 |
| virginica | 0 | 1 | 9 |


