\image(figures/xgb_iris_test.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Final test metrics and confusion counts after refitting on all development data. The left panel projects four-feature predictions onto petal coordinates and marks errors in black. The right panel is a training-derived importance summary.)
\alttext(A species-coloured test scatter with two black error markers beside a gain-importance bar chart dominated by petal measurements.)

The reserved test result is 28 correct predictions out of 30: accuracy
0.93333, mean log loss 0.22392 and multiclass Brier score 0.12682. One
versicolor and one virginica are exchanged. The refitted model classifies
all 120 development flowers correctly, illustrating why training accuracy
is not a replacement for the held-out result. A single flower changes this
test accuracy by 3.33 percentage points, and one split does not quantify
variation over other splits or populations. Softmax probabilities have not
been separately calibrated.

> Computational and modelling limits

The local implementation enumerates midpoint candidates and rescans their
rows. A conservative bound is \(O(MK(p+1)n^2(D+1))\) training work for \(M\)
rounds, \(K\) classes, \(p\) resolved predictors and depth limit \(D\).
Staged evaluation traverses each stored tree once per usable evaluation
row, with \(O(nMK(D+1))\) traversal and \(O(nMK)\) score work. It avoids
refitting \(M\) prefix models. This small exact-search implementation is
appropriate for understanding the method; it does not implement upstream
XGBoost's full scalable, sparse or distributed training system.

Null response or predictor rows are omitted and counted consistently;
unseen response classes and non-finite transformed predictors are errors.
Categorical predictor levels must have appeared during training. Formula
basis construction belongs to the training design, which is reused for
validation and prediction. The procedure therefore separates fitting and
scoring, but the user remains responsible for a sampling design relevant
to the intended population and for keeping partitions disjoint.

> Conclusion

The regularised leaf optimum and split gain follow from a quadratic loss
surrogate; the diagonal multiclass approximation and curvature floor are
explicit implementation choices. Staged evaluation shows training loss
continuing to improve after validation loss has stopped improving.
Validation selects a penalty and stopping round, then a fresh development
fit is scored on reserved flowers. This sequence supports a measured
predictive claim while keeping model selection, training fit and final
evaluation distinct.

> References

- T. Chen and C. Guestrin (2016). *XGBoost: A Scalable Tree Boosting System*.
  KDD, 785–794. https://doi.org/10.1145/2939672.2939785
  Author manuscript: https://arxiv.org/abs/1603.02754
- XGBoost 3.1.0 multiclass objective, including its Hessian bound:
  https://github.com/dmlc/xgboost/blob/v3.1.0/src/objective/multiclass_obj.cu
- G. W. Brier (1950). *Verification of Forecasts Expressed in Terms of Probability*.
  Monthly Weather Review 78(1), 1–3.
  https://journals.ametsoc.org/view/journals/mwre/78/1/1520-0493_1950_078_0001_vofeit_2_0_co_2.xml
- R. A. Fisher (1936). *The Use of Multiple Measurements in Taxonomic Problems*.
  Annals of Eugenics 7(2), 179–188. https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1469-1809.1936.tb02137.x
